    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1008">

                <pb facs="0946" n="938" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg938"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/2412: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-06-20T18:00:00-04:00">June 20, 1941—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>95. Your 160, May 22, 3 p.m. In the course of a recent discussion with an officer
                    of the Department regarding the possibility of using Turkish ships to transport
                    chrome to Red Sea ports, the Turkish Ambassador<anchor n="23" xml:id="d1008-f1"
                    /> said he could see no reason why some such arrangement could not be made. He
                    added that he would be glad to give us the names of the American vessels which
                    are transporting goods for Turkey to Port Suez in the thought that if this
                    information were transmitted to our Embassy at Ankara, arrangements could be
                    worked out there for Turkish ships to carry chrome to Port Suez and pick up the
                    cargo from the United States. He said he would be very glad to cooperate in this
                    manner in any way he could since it seemed to him that it was to the mutual
                    advantage of both Governments.</p>
                <p>Please telegraph your comments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1008-f1" n="23" xml:id="d1008-f1-content">
